\boxed{Africa}

Africa was historically referred to as the "Dark Continent" by European explorers and writers in the 19th century, notably popularized by Henry Morton Stanley. The term arose largely because much of Africa's interior was unmapped and unknown to Europeans at the time, though it also carried misleading and derogatory connotations about the continent. Today, the phrase is widely considered outdated and offensive.

