Looking at this question about Homo naledi from the Rising Star cave system:

Homo naledi is controversial primarily because it displays a mosaic of features that complicates our understanding of hominid evolution—having a very small brain size (~465-610 cc) and primitive, Australopithecus-like post-cranial features alongside more modern, Homo-like traits (hands and feet adapted for tool use and bipedalism). This combination challenges the linear progression model where derived traits are expected to appear together. While dating difficulties were initially a significant issue, the enduring controversy centers on how these mixed traits fit (or don't fit) into established evolutionary frameworks.

